# This file is injected into init.rc by KernelSU (initrc/ in a module dir).
#
# It does one thing: make sure the FunctionFS function instance and its link exist BEFORE
# anything binds the gadget to a UDC. configfs refuses to add a function link once
# gi->composite.gadget_driver.udc_name is set (drivers/usb/gadget/configfs.c:473,
# config_usb_cfg_link returns -EINVAL), and the vendor's own rc creates the gadget in
# "on early-boot", which runs AFTER post-fs-data.
#
# Only the minimum is written here. Everything else of the gadget skeleton (idVendor, idProduct,
# strings, config attributes, os_desc) is written later by the vendor rc, whose mkdir is idempotent:
# init's do_mkdir() lstats first and accepts an existing directory, and a failing symlink is logged
# and skipped. The framework's gadget HAL starts after early-boot, so it finds a complete gadget.
#
# The instance is functions/ffs.ira: configfs strips the "ffs." prefix and hands "ira" to
# ffs_set_inst_name() (configfs.c:661 -> f_fs.c:3576), which is the name the daemon mounts
# ("mount -t functionfs ira <instance dir>"). The daemon writes the descriptors and strings.
#
# The link is named "ira" on purpose: the HAL's unlinkFunctions() only removes entries whose name
# contains "function", and the kernel only refuses a *duplicate of the same instance*, so our
# functions/ffs.ira link neither collides with the HAL's functions/ffs.adb nor is ever removed by it.
#
# The name does not decide interface order. configfs appends every link to the configuration's
# function list (config_usb_cfg_link: list_add_tail) and the composite binds that list in order
# (configfs_composite_bind -> usb_add_function); the descriptor order is that list. Names are never
# compared.
#
# Because the framework links its own functions/ffs.adb into this same configuration later, the
# descriptor set the daemon writes cannot be decided here or at mount. It follows the link set and
# is re-evaluated on every bind and on every link change (src/adbd/ira_ffs.cpp): with ffs.adb
# absent the daemon presents the adb interface (ff/42/01, ep1+ep2), and with it present it presents
# an inert ff/00/00 interface with no endpoints and leaves adb to the platform's function.

on post-fs-data
    mkdir /config/usb_gadget/g1 0755 root root
    mkdir /config/usb_gadget/g1/functions 0755 root root
    mkdir /config/usb_gadget/g1/configs 0755 root root
    mkdir /config/usb_gadget/g1/configs/b.1 0755 root root
    mkdir /config/usb_gadget/g1/functions/ffs.ira 0755 root root
    symlink /config/usb_gadget/g1/functions/ffs.ira /config/usb_gadget/g1/configs/b.1/ira
